tes matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aef89aa-352b-5bfd-b894-d5a6aa700de7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392059} {:answer/id #uuid "35c2dbc4-a502-58b1-b028-c6066cf70d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392060} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc2efdf-e18a-58d8-9ad6-5bdf0228a9fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392061} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccf6827-947c-53f9-9bbb-08f0c53653fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392062} {:answer/id #uuid "289737b2-0d6d-5390-9fae-aa676b2d8f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "289737b2-0d6d-5390-9fae-aa676b2d8f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392063} :answer-field/id #uuid "e30c4e35-75bd-5c3f-9c45-ae4bf9b81adc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "894b16d3-3e6a-5bd5-b6ff-86b2b95aa0cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-37154" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=-3{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=-3{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75f99091-153d-58b0-8d7a-f2ce8ee1e8a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394396} {:answer/id #uuid "863492c4-8771-5d99-8c0b-6c0aeb191f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394397} {:answer/id #uuid "b12b4d58-6386-5f7d-b39a-aea590a0725e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394398} {:answer/id #uuid "715b88bb-ce67-5d5c-8fb9-a903eac314f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-17 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394399} {:answer/id #uuid "e03b8647-5994-5de7-9312-aa41c2444b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-