} {:answer/id #uuid "45687a63-f60a-5bfb-a261-9e94c6a4c076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440414} {:answer/id #uuid "796a9b6a-3558-5233-a3ac-8460d1cea27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440428} {:answer/id #uuid "cb21b0ff-7e60-5f91-938c-d9d9d7cbf7a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440440} {:answer/id #uuid "cc371831-0ddf-5330-a6c1-805660c3647a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45687a63-f60a-5bfb-a261-9e94c6a4c076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440414} :answer-field/id #uuid "549d99dc-683d-5071-97ad-668e834f5eb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9bc30c67-14a3-514e-87f9-9c3583feaf85" :question/math-academy-id "q-82601" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b1e14eb-c6fe-5d5e-a0dc-b061059559c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394416} {:answer/id #uuid "985b430f-4b57-5849-b48f-23adc660de97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394417} {:answer/id #uuid "dab5b566-c168-514d-b3c6-1c83c5b47cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394418} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a4ab04-a137-58d3-a387-4f383d0ae878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394419} {:answer/id #uuid "71cfe1b4-e7cb-5edf-9ce0-5c9a0fe890fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "985b430f-4b57-5849-b48f-23adc660de97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394417} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7cbaef8-14a8-5424-bff9-cdd4ca81b618" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c7d63da-59ab-5308-9b89-bf650fb9db9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-82606" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 9}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84bf4f13-8394-5e74-8611-52bcbe63b443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394423} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f43405-b85b-5f16-ae6f-5e4abfc15221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394424} {:answer/id #uuid "1be8e3f6-f794-5e2a-9dc7-026e640b732f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394425} {:answer/id #uuid "924d16bd-723c-52e2-9669-dce0fa52dce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394426} {:answer/id #uuid "80c96893-628f-5b23-b717-b15529348c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "924d16bd-723c-52e2-9669-dce0fa52dce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394426} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dc08976-f574-52b6-a28f-57c50285b1e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13aef9df-8682-5fe0-a906-e0817933e236" :question/math-academy-id "q-82609" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-4)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124577 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a356bf6-c5f6-5626-b37d-d3aa5d04ec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349277} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1ff8f8-bc39-524a-8272-e771ca3767b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -29\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349849} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4fcc3c-344a-5fe2-a3f1-717dc5d18898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350145} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcd859b-049f-5e51-885f-b10217b48fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350241} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9f3522-d091-5f2a-a8c4-a46fc74e67fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a356bf6-c5f6-5626-b37d-d3aa5d04ec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349277} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fa41aa0-25a9-5a0a-b9d6-671a033593b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "77f1fca8-2c83-502e-92f3-c4632be6ced3" :question/math-academy-id "q-82618" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 18 & -29 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}18 & -29 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -29 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124577 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18dc48a1-5985-578f-8b5e-9016d734956d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440404} {:answer/id #uuid "1b76988c-1557-52a5-91a3-c4a65053602e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440405} {:answer/id #uuid "1bceb481-65f5-5e67-97b5-3ca99d15ac64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440406} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1b735c-b2d7-53c7-85cc-47c1e2305f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440409} {:answer/id #uuid "9d360efc-d5a1-5ea4-8321-aa2b837cea50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18dc48a1-5985-578f-8b5e-9016d734956d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440404} :answer-field/id #uuid "4922efa6-bafc-57b9-a817-72a740df2719" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "92fb3400-1e9e-5f15-b0ad-6ea07e725536" :question/math-academy-id "q-82620" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 13 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 13 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 13 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4616c187-092f-502c-9325-a8e520cc53fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440415} {:a