}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "872a1d84-7604-5644-bc38-442784c11eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394437} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a28644-53a1-5a38-bba8-149b3d772482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394438} {:answer/id #uuid "64316907-421b-5225-a825-ede2ef831ce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394439} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7a4288-c4eb-550f-baf9-7134d0851825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394440} {:answer/id #uuid "a32654b0-0db8-5775-ad24-72aac7cebc3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64316907-421b-5225-a825-ede2ef831ce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394439} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c07407-f730-50b5-83dd-936494ac4aab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57c0c3fb-cd0e-588f-875c-0ee340ecbc73" :question/math-academy-id "q-82702" :question/problem "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=3{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $x=3{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05a2a04d-6fe7-5a0d-8718-80558dc272b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -13\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440398} {:answer/id #uuid "4b07ccd2-15b5-5fe4-9051-5d908e60ebd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440417} {:answer/id #uuid "50b3b88a-a623-50e5-b968-d9227a27e597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440420} {:answer/id #uuid "c51f010d-b055-55be-a901-8dda67213b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440438} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa60034-0dcd-5f83-8b52-44d789dd2759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05a2a04d-6fe7-5a0d-8718-80558dc272b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -13\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440398} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca6335cf-a241-5413-8b14-741fb6ccabc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50a9b032-ab11-5594-a8b4-f98e14bf6ea6" :question/math-academy-id "q-82706" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 7\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 7-12\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "549a0eee-e056-5b3e-945c-dc08eab7d67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394444} {:answer/id #uuid "780e5eeb-967c-58e6-9b97-e28954821893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394445} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbfa154-65b9-54ed-a535-feab787fda53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394446} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d33501-64aa-5fb9-b7f1-cd2d776eee3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394447} {:answer/id #uuid "48d8e050-c67b-591d-a02d-6b6dede8752f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fbfa154-65b9-54ed-a535-feab787fda53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394446} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2130ea6-8c98-546a-8865-99cd9a3a8e9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a7ee4f4-73c6-5824-a327-bfe7ce4e304c" :question/math-academy-id "q-82707" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 10}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "339eb9d7-c3ff-5722-b2a4-62c01e506dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417130} {:answer/id #uuid "7545326f-2ed3-5b45-b83f-fdce64ffd43b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417142} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ab3da6-d52a-52fe-8824-525245387581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417162} {:answer/id #uuid "f99a0a3e-8e69-5c5a-8d55-fb74464b4d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417171} {:answer/id #uuid "fa770ac4-af40-5a3c-b0e8-fb5ff74934d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa770ac4-af40-5a3c-b0e8-fb5ff74934d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417172} :answer-field/id #uuid "61a8f832-f2b3-5123-8233-870da3bb21e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15cdd9a6-5092-51ed-93a5-b2dd11d2ff12" :question/math-academy-id "q-82713" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
